'' linux-x86_64: a BYVAL cva_list is the CALLER'S LIST, by address. SysV's va_list is an array,
'' so C's 'void f(int, va_list)' takes a pointer and its va_arg advances the caller's record;
'' fbc passes the pointer too. tiko copied the 24-byte record ('ptr byval([24 x i8])') and a C
'' reader handed it segfaulted (build/aapcslane.sh t_va3, 2026-11-19). Pinned: the definition
'' and the declaration take a plain 'ptr', a direct call and a call through a procedure
'' pointer hand over the list's own slot with no copy, and the callee passes its pointer on.
'' cva_arg through it is clang's register-save-area sequence (gp_offset < 41), not LLVM's
'' 'va_arg', which -O0 miscompiles when the list is not a stack slot (llvmaggcopy.tiko).
'' Run under WSL at -O0 and -O1 against clang-18 callers and callees, 2026-11-19.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -target linux-x86_64 --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/cva-list-byval.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: define i64 @"TK_VSUM"(i32 %p0, ptr %p1)
'' EXPECT-OUT: .inreg = icmp ult i32
'' EXPECT-OUT: call void @"C_VREAD"(i32 %t23, ptr %t22)
'' EXPECT-OUT: call i64 @"TK_VSUM"(i32 %t8, ptr %s2)
'' EXPECT-OUT: call void %t13(i32 %t12, ptr %s2)
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare void @"C_VREAD"(i32, ptr)
'' EXPECT-NOT: byval(
'' EXPECT-NOT: va_arg ptr
